1
00:00:01,058 --> 00:00:02,133
More than eleven years

2
00:00:02,217 --> 00:00:04,700
in talent acquisition have taught me one thing:

3
00:00:04,783 --> 00:00:06,458
great hiring is about judgment,

4
00:00:06,542 --> 00:00:08,450
not volume. And that led me to a question

5
00:00:08,533 --> 00:00:10,325
that has driven my work ever since.

6
00:00:10,408 --> 00:00:12,298
What if that judgment could scale?

7
00:00:13,792 --> 00:00:15,550
Let me start with who I am.

8
00:00:15,633 --> 00:00:18,083
I lead talent acquisition in Ho Chi Minh City,

9
00:00:18,167 --> 00:00:19,100
working in Vietnamese,

10
00:00:19,183 --> 00:00:20,350
English and Japanese.

11
00:00:22,150 --> 00:00:24,125
My path here has given me a wide view

12
00:00:24,208 --> 00:00:25,700
of people and business.

13
00:00:25,783 --> 00:00:28,392
I began in banking, on the international settlement desk.

14
00:00:28,475 --> 00:00:30,975
I then built my career in talent acquisition:

15
00:00:31,067 --> 00:00:33,325
I ran an IT branch of around thirty people

16
00:00:33,408 --> 00:00:34,642
for a Japanese company,

17
00:00:34,725 --> 00:00:37,450
and spent five years as an HR and Recruiting Manager.

18
00:00:37,533 --> 00:00:39,542
Today, I recruit senior technology talent

19
00:00:39,625 --> 00:00:41,608
for a US executive search firm.

20
00:00:41,692 --> 00:00:43,342
I have also presented at industry events

21
00:00:43,425 --> 00:00:46,200
on how AI can be applied in HR and recruitment.

22
00:00:48,075 --> 00:00:49,483
Across all of those roles,

23
00:00:49,567 --> 00:00:50,917
one lesson kept coming back.

24
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:52,483
A CV records the past,

25
00:00:52,567 --> 00:00:53,433
but never the whole person.

26
00:00:53,517 --> 00:00:55,200
So before any search begins,

27
00:00:55,283 --> 00:00:56,950
I define the role with the client:

28
00:00:57,033 --> 00:00:58,217
the must-have skills, the team,

29
00:00:58,300 --> 00:01:00,367
and the manager the new hire will work with.

30
00:01:00,450 --> 00:01:01,983
Getting this right first saves weeks

31
00:01:02,067 --> 00:01:04,067
of searching in the wrong direction.

32
00:01:05,525 --> 00:01:06,717
Once the role is clear,

33
00:01:06,800 --> 00:01:09,117
the search begins. I approach talent directly,

34
00:01:09,200 --> 00:01:10,900
including people who are not yet looking,

35
00:01:10,983 --> 00:01:12,567
and interview each candidate using

36
00:01:12,650 --> 00:01:14,508
the hiring manager's own criteria.

37
00:01:14,592 --> 00:01:16,217
Out of twenty to forty candidates,

38
00:01:16,300 --> 00:01:17,858
only one or two are presented,

39
00:01:17,942 --> 00:01:19,550
each with a clear reason why they fit.

40
00:01:19,633 --> 00:01:21,683
That way, leaders spend their time only

41
00:01:21,767 --> 00:01:23,100
on people worth meeting.

42
00:01:23,183 --> 00:01:24,600
Over ten years, this approach has led

43
00:01:24,683 --> 00:01:26,067
to more than eight hundred offers,

44
00:01:26,150 --> 00:01:29,058
from junior and senior engineers to team leads and managers,

45
00:01:29,142 --> 00:01:32,708
backed by an IT talent database of nearly five thousand CVs.

46
00:01:33,992 --> 00:01:35,433
To make this repeatable,

47
00:01:35,517 --> 00:01:36,767
I built a method of my own:

48
00:01:36,850 --> 00:01:38,925
a twenty-three-step recruitment workflow,

49
00:01:39,008 --> 00:01:41,883
with checklists, metrics and a report at every milestone.

50
00:01:41,967 --> 00:01:43,533
And it does not stop at the offer.

51
00:01:43,617 --> 00:01:45,367
I follow each placement through probation,

52
00:01:45,450 --> 00:01:47,050
because a hire only succeeds

53
00:01:47,133 --> 00:01:48,925
when the person stays and grows.

54
00:01:50,692 --> 00:01:53,583
But a process run by hand can only grow so far.

55
00:01:53,667 --> 00:01:55,506
Good candidates wait too long for a reply,

56
00:01:55,589 --> 00:01:57,983
follow-ups slip, and valuable judgment is spent

57
00:01:58,067 --> 00:01:59,100
on repetitive work.

58
00:01:59,183 --> 00:02:01,717
With years beside engineers and years in HR,

59
00:02:01,800 --> 00:02:03,183
I built the system myself,

60
00:02:03,267 --> 00:02:05,683
with one purpose: let software carry the routine,

61
00:02:05,767 --> 00:02:07,708
so people can focus on decisions.

62
00:02:09,492 --> 00:02:11,867
The first thing I built was an app that signs in

63
00:02:11,950 --> 00:02:14,117
to many recruiter accounts from a single screen,

64
00:02:14,200 --> 00:02:17,633
and uses an AI agent to grow the network across all of them.

65
00:02:17,717 --> 00:02:19,200
The agent is trained to reply just

66
00:02:19,283 --> 00:02:20,283
as a real recruiter would,

67
00:02:20,367 --> 00:02:22,033
day and night, in any language,

68
00:02:22,117 --> 00:02:24,475
and no candidate is ever contacted twice.

69
00:02:24,558 --> 00:02:27,567
The purpose: no good candidate is missed or kept waiting,

70
00:02:27,650 --> 00:02:30,372
and one recruiter reaches as far as a whole team.

71
00:02:31,883 --> 00:02:33,408
Behind every conversation,

72
00:02:33,492 --> 00:02:34,725
it reports back to me,

73
00:02:34,808 --> 00:02:35,792
candidate by candidate:

74
00:02:35,875 --> 00:02:38,508
their potential, how well they fit each open role,

75
00:02:38,592 --> 00:02:40,925
and how that score changes with everything they share.

76
00:02:41,008 --> 00:02:42,958
When the fit is strong and the salary matches,

77
00:02:43,042 --> 00:02:44,092
it books a call with me,

78
00:02:44,175 --> 00:02:46,108
so I can interview the candidate myself.

79
00:02:46,192 --> 00:02:48,025
Instead of reading thousands of messages,

80
00:02:48,108 --> 00:02:49,225
I read one clear report,

81
00:02:49,308 --> 00:02:51,919
and spend my time on the candidates who matter.

82
00:02:53,358 --> 00:02:55,517
It also reviews its own work every day,

83
00:02:55,600 --> 00:02:57,025
learning from each conversation,

84
00:02:57,108 --> 00:02:58,183
correcting its mistakes,

85
00:02:58,267 --> 00:02:59,767
and proposing improvements that I approve,

86
00:02:59,850 --> 00:03:01,567
so its quality grows every week

87
00:03:01,650 --> 00:03:03,358
while a person stays in control.

88
00:03:03,442 --> 00:03:04,742
And every action is measured.

89
00:03:04,825 --> 00:03:06,022
Every thirty days, on average:

90
00:03:06,105 --> 00:03:07,098
one hundred and ninety CVs,

91
00:03:07,182 --> 00:03:09,433
one hundred and sixty top-tier candidates,

92
00:03:09,517 --> 00:03:11,742
and one hundred and fifty interview calls.

93
00:03:11,825 --> 00:03:13,283
A sourcing team that never stops,

94
00:03:13,367 --> 00:03:14,433
and reports in numbers,

95
00:03:14,517 --> 00:03:15,517
not feelings.

96
00:03:17,275 --> 00:03:20,192
The same idea now runs through the rest of HR.

97
00:03:20,275 --> 00:03:22,517
I built a virtual office that records remote work

98
00:03:22,600 --> 00:03:24,333
and calculates timesheets automatically,

99
00:03:24,417 --> 00:03:27,508
so managers see real working time without paperwork.

100
00:03:27,592 --> 00:03:29,367
A chat platform keeps every candidate

101
00:03:29,450 --> 00:03:31,367
conversation inside the company,

102
00:03:31,450 --> 00:03:33,667
so knowledge stays when people move on.

103
00:03:33,750 --> 00:03:35,017
And an applicant tracking system

104
00:03:35,100 --> 00:03:37,217
scores each CV against the job,

105
00:03:37,300 --> 00:03:39,333
so screening is faster and fairer.

106
00:03:39,417 --> 00:03:40,600
The goal behind all of it:

107
00:03:40,683 --> 00:03:43,850
HR that runs on data, and people free for the human work.

108
00:03:45,450 --> 00:03:47,825
Still, in HR, the most important thing is not

109
00:03:47,908 --> 00:03:49,217
to automate everything.

110
00:03:49,300 --> 00:03:50,342
Hiring is about people.

111
00:03:50,425 --> 00:03:52,925
For senior roles, I believe that meeting candidates

112
00:03:53,008 --> 00:03:55,775
in person is the foundation of truly understanding them:

113
00:03:55,858 --> 00:03:57,658
their motivations, their doubts,

114
00:03:57,742 --> 00:03:59,025
and the way they lead others.

115
00:03:59,108 --> 00:04:01,058
These are things no software can capture.

116
00:04:01,142 --> 00:04:03,542
So I interview every senior candidate face to face,

117
00:04:03,625 --> 00:04:05,375
from team leads to CTOs.

118
00:04:05,458 --> 00:04:07,742
I go to meet them, listen to what really drives them,

119
00:04:07,825 --> 00:04:09,769
and negotiate each offer in person.

120
00:04:11,450 --> 00:04:13,817
That is the balance I bring to every search.

121
00:04:13,900 --> 00:04:15,075
AI handles the volume,

122
00:04:15,158 --> 00:04:16,942
so no good candidate is missed.

123
00:04:17,025 --> 00:04:19,875
I bring the judgment, built in face-to-face conversations,

124
00:04:19,958 --> 00:04:21,425
so the right person is chosen.

125
00:04:21,508 --> 00:04:22,958
Together, they help companies hire

126
00:04:23,042 --> 00:04:24,925
senior people who stay and grow.

127
00:04:25,008 --> 00:04:27,842
I would be glad to talk about the next senior hire.

